{% decision_card number="ADR-0017" status="approved" %}
Phase 1.5 platform-completion boundary approved 2026-08-09.
{% /decision_card %}

## Context

Beacon has a fixed eight-role Claude subagent set, a compact context primer, a narrow token-auditor pilot, provider-neutral orchestration domain types, deterministic repository quality gates, and an immutable build-and-promotion path. These are useful foundations, but they do not yet form a measurable agent platform contract.

Agent definitions can drift without dedicated CI. Token and context claims are not backed by an automatic evidence stream. Routing rules lack a Beacon-owned benchmark. Prompt assembly has no enforced role budget. Prompt-level permission guidance is not a deterministic tool-policy boundary. Agent-run evidence is not yet correlated through the release lifecycle.

Expanding into CRM, customer portal, billing, project-management, or major UI work while these controls are still moving would mix platform experimentation into every product feature.

## Decision drivers

- make all eight role contracts machine-validatable and resistant to drift;
- measure token, cache, context, latency, retry, routing, and quality outcomes before optimizing them;
- preserve the same approved task meaning across Claude and Codex;
- make tool safety and blocking gates deterministic rather than advisory;
- connect agent-run evidence to build, promotion, verification, and rollback evidence;
- avoid embeddings, vector storage, learned routing, and other infrastructure until Beacon-owned data proves a recurring need;
- keep the current orchestration module truthful as a simulation until live execution is separately authorized.

## Options considered

- continue major product and UI expansion while improving agent tooling opportunistically;
- replace the current role set and orchestration simulation with a large autonomous-agent framework;
- complete a bounded Phase 1.5 engineering control plane, pass measurable gates, freeze the contract, then resume business expansion;
- immediately build embeddings, full RAG, a vector database, graph indexing, semantic caching, and a learned router.

## Decision

Adopt Phase 1.5 as the current repository phase and finish the engineering agent platform before major business-domain or UI expansion becomes the primary focus.

Build now:

- one machine-readable manifest for the fixed eight-role set and deterministic CI validation against the human-readable role files;
- automatic run and token telemetry with role, provider, model, context, cache, output, latency, retry, routing, and quality fields;
- bounded context selection and provider-specific prompt compilation from the same canonical work request;
- an explainable Claude/Codex routing benchmark with capacity and failure overrides;
- deterministic pre-tool policy evaluation that returns allow, ask, or deny;
- functional, security, handoff, failure, budget, and routing evaluation fixtures;
- scoped deterministic agent CI plus visible, separately triggered live-provider evaluations;
- one correlated evidence lifecycle from agent run through immutable artifact, staging, production authorization, verification, and rollback identity.

Use provider-native prompt caching when available, but record actual cache evidence before making savings claims. Continue rule-based routing until Beacon owns a sufficiently large labeled outcome set.

Reserve extension interfaces, but do not implement embeddings, a vector database, full RAG, graph indexing, a semantic cache, or fine-tuned routing during Phase 1.5 without a new evidence-backed decision.

Phase 1.5 authorizes only controlled, credential-gated engineering eval invocation through the provider adapter boundary. It does not authorize an autonomous business runtime, durable business queue, database, automation workers, admin dashboard, customer portal, or billing.

The platform completion gate requires validated contracts, deterministic agent regression checks, explicit and logged provider routing, real token/context evidence, bounded repeatable context, quality-preserving optimization, deterministic safety enforcement, controlled execution scope, independent QA/review, non-overridable blocking gates, correlated evidence, immutable release identity with proven rollback, and documentation that matches the implementation. Components existing without this evidence do not pass the gate.

## Implementation status

Milestone 1 is implemented: the phase decision, synchronized canonical docs, fixed eight-role manifest, source hashes, validator, tests, and CI wiring exist.

PR-A adds the telemetry recording foundation: strict `AgentRun` and `ProviderRun` runtime/JSON schemas, Anthropic and OpenAI usage normalization, bounded provider-metadata redaction, ignored append-only local NDJSON, a redacted CI evidence sink, a recording CLI, and deterministic schema/redaction/normalization/failure tests. This is not evidence of real live-provider runs. Representative live records and automatic adapter population remain required during the later provider-execution milestone before the telemetry completion gate can pass.

PR-B through PR-I are implemented locally: deterministic context preflight/compiler, conditional token-auditor policy, sixteen-family eval harness, role-aware tool gateway and Claude hook, Claude/OpenAI/Codex CLI adapters, live-work-unit evidence flow, risk-tiered council, evidence-gated tuning, diff-bound publication policy, dedicated agent CI, GitHub provenance generation/verification, same-artifact promotion, environment-specific verification, and a known-good rollback workflow.

PR-J is an executable completion audit. Its local-only mode passes. Full closure remains blocked by representative live run/baseline evidence, the new required server check, non-bypassable external production approval, a real verified attestation, staging and production verification, and a rollback drill. An approved Codex CLI smoke timed out without events and produced no record; it does not satisfy the live gate.

## Positive consequences

- business features can rely on a stable, tested engineering factory;
- role, prompt, routing, context, and permission drift becomes reviewable;
- cost reductions must remain coupled to quality and security outcomes;
- Claude and Codex remain interchangeable at the task-contract boundary;
- advanced retrieval and routing infrastructure is deferred until justified;
- the distinction between engineering tooling and the simulated business broker remains explicit.

## Negative consequences

- major business-domain and UI work is intentionally delayed;
- agent-platform changes add CI maintenance and benchmark cost;
- live evaluations consume provider budget and therefore cannot run on every unrelated pull request;
- initial context budgets and routing rules will need adjustment as telemetry accumulates;
- the solo operation still cannot claim independent human review.

## Risks

- the phase could become open-ended unless each completion-gate result has executable evidence;
- telemetry could collect secrets, PII, or unnecessary prompt content unless records stay metadata-only and redacted;
- a rule-based router can encode preference without sufficient outcome data;
- a policy gateway can be bypassed if it evaluates raw command strings instead of normalized actions;
- documentation may overstate live execution when the current broker is still a simulation;
- generated evidence can become unauditable if run, commit, artifact, deployment, and rollback identifiers are not correlated.

## Follow-up work

1. Produce a successful representative live provider run and repeated benchmark baseline.
2. Run the new agent-platform job and add its exact check context to the protected-main ruleset.
3. Configure a non-bypassable external production reviewer gate and the agent-evals environment.
4. Generate and verify a real build attestation, then promote and verify the same artifact through staging and production.
5. Execute the known-good rollback drill and correlate its evidence.
6. Run `npm run phase15:audit`; freeze only when it passes every hard gate.

## Supersedes or superseded by

New decision; superseded by: none. It extends ADR-0016 from a narrow pilot into an evidence-gated platform-completion phase without superseding the pilot's build-later boundary.

